WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:02.920
- [Narrator] Scientists in
South Africa were the first

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:04.740
to raise alarms about Omicron.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.200
And virologists in this lab
have been leading the way

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.710
on researching the fast spreading variant.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:12.390
- The variant has been
studied in South Africa

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.810
longer than it has been
studied in other places.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:18.660
And this lets us understand it better

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.110
and enables us to kind of
inform the rest of the world.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.210
- [Narrator] These researchers
are pooling their findings

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.670
with results from other
labs around the world

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.330
and a clearer picture of
Omicron characteristics

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.180
has begun to emerge.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.180
They say the strain possibly
causes milder symptoms

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:37.420
but is more resistant to
vaccines and more infectious.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.110
- Early data suggest that
Omicron is more transmissible

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:46.090
than Delta with a doubling
time of about two days.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:47.610
- [Narrator] As the
variant poses a challenge

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:49.000
for governments everywhere,

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.790
we visited this lab to understand

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.053
what scientists have
learned about Omicron.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:57.370
Alex Sigal and his team of virologists

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.370
tested the new strain of the
virus against the Pfizer shot.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.490
- So I'm preparing the cells
for infection tomorrow.

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:06.089
So it's all starting here.

00:01:06.089 --> 00:01:07.410
- [Narrator] Their analysis
showed that the variant

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.710
can partially evade
protection from vaccines

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.380
or prior immunity.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:14.630
- The fact that it's more
likely to infect people

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:18.140
who are vaccinated but
it's less likely to cause

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.810
serious illness compared
to the other variants

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:24.250
is exactly what we see
in our work as well.

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.110
- [Narrator] Their research
predicted that for people

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.200
who have been vaccinated,
effectiveness against infection

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:33.820
is 35% compared with 72% for
people who have been vaccinated

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:37.010
and who have immunity
from a prior infection.

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:38.480
These findings were roughly in line

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.040
with another recent real-world study.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.220
But it's difficult to
draw broad conclusions

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:46.430
from South Africa, which
compared with Europe and the US

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.230
has a younger population.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.680
It also has a relatively
low vaccination rate

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.480
as compared with places like the UK.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.010
While there's still limited data,

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.480
Sigal's team has also
observed that symptoms

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.190
from this strain might be less severe.

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.120
Another group of
researchers in South Africa

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.770
found that even though the
number of cases is rising,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.880
the risk of infection
of hospital admission

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.780
for infected people is 29% lower

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.930
compared with the first wave in 2020.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:16.930
- So far the infections have
been mild, mostly mild here

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.100
and there might be
several reasons for that.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:24.100
One reason is there's a lot
more pre-existing immunity.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.850
There are a lot more people
who are previously infected

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:31.160
with a different variants
before Omicron came on the scene

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.810
and also considerable number
of people now in South Africa

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.640
who were vaccinated,
that wasn't here before.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:37.780
But there might be other reasons.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.740
Perhaps the virus itself
is a little bit different.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.633
This we don't know yet.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:46.410
- [Narrator] Even if the
variant might be less virulent,

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.760
the sheer number of cases could still lead

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.620
to a surge in hospitalizations
as the strain seems to be

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:53.560
far more transmissible than others.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.330
- For a variant like Omicron,

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.600
it's kind of a Frankenstein's
monster of mutation.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:02:59.700
It's heavily mutated.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.310
What that means is it
gains certain abilities.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:07.280
One of these abilities is to
escape from an immune response

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.810
to a previous infection or to vaccination.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.000
And the second ability
is to transmit better.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.660
- [Narrator] That's prompting
governments in the US

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.970
and Europe to accelerate
roll-outs of booster shots.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.600
Early studies have suggested
that another Pfizer shot

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.950
will increase protection against Omicron.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.350
But the majority of the world's

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:28.470
still hasn't been fully vaccinated.

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.500
And South Africa only begins it's rollout

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.970
of booster shots in January.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.940
Global leaders are now
weighing the evidence

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.060
as they decide what policies to implement

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.500
and how to best protect their populations.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.070
- Everybody is of course worried

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:43.700
and they wanna predict
what is gonna happen.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.780
So it's a very much a
globalized effort now.

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:49.660
In South Africa, we happen to have

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.000
a very good research community
for infectious disease

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.440
and we're able to make a lot of progress

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.240
because also, it started
here before elsewhere

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.800
and we able to say something
to the rest of the world

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.143
and give some predictions.

